% warrants.bib — the claims of paperkit's front-door doc.  This README is itself
% a paperkit projection of THIS file (root paper.toml: out = README.md).  Every
% example is a CITED claim that also emits its on-disk asset (emit = …): the prose
% introduces and cites it by key in square brackets, the block renders below it.
% An emit with no claim would be an UNCITED placement — a postulate — so there are
% none here.
%
% Ζ·bib·strict — NO BARE AT-SIGN OUTSIDE AN ENTRY, not even inside a comment.
% The percent sign is a TeX convention paperkit adopted; real BibTeX has no comment
% syntax at all — text between entries is simply ignored.  But an at-sign in that
% text STARTS an entry, and a conforming parser then demands a brace or paren.
% Measured against two independent oracles: pybtex REFUSES this entire file over a
% single bracketed citation written up here, while bibtexparser accepts it — the
% divergence is real, and only the SECOND oracle found it.
%
% ⚑ THE PERCENT SIGN IS NOT THE DEFECT.  Dividers carrying no at-sign parse cleanly
% in both, and so does an at-sign INSIDE a value (a citation in a claim, an email
% address).  So the rule is exactly "no bare at-sign outside an entry body" — where
% "drop the percent sign" would have edited every comment in the corpus and fixed
% nothing, since unmarked prose refuses identically.
%
% ⚑ AND THIS BLOCK CARRIED A COUNT, WHICH WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR.  It said "keeps
% all 161 comments"; the census found 180, and the divider strip removed 28 of them.
% A number in prose beside a set nobody counts is the furniture this corpus has an
% entry about (result-tristate.bib: "a verified claim can carry unverified furniture,
% and PROSE is where it rides").  The count is gone; the rule does not need one.
%
% ⚑ And this comment is its own first test: an earlier draft explained the rule
% using the character it forbids, taking the file from 2 offending lines to 7.

